# .trivyignore — list of trivy findings to suppress, with rationale.
#
# Format: one finding per line, identified by the misconfig ID. Comments
# on the same line explain why we suppress it. Trivy ignores findings
# whose ID appears in this file (with a comment).
#
# Reference: https://aquasecurity.github.io/trivy/v0.71/docs/scanner/vulnerability/#ignoring-findings
# (for .trivyignore; the misconfig-suppression syntax is the same)

# DS-0002 (HIGH): "Image user should not be 'root'"
# AWARE-Dockerfile.training: deliberate design choice. The training
# image runs as root inside an ephemeral Modal GPU pod where:
#   - the network is namespaced by Modal,
#   - the only mounted volume is the per-job `aware-training-data`
#     Volume (no other containers share it),
#   - the job process exits within 5 hours (timeout 18000s).
# Running as root is the v1.0.0 baseline and is documented inline
# in the Dockerfile ("SECURITY" section). Changing to a non-root
# user would require (a) creating a user, (b) chown'ing /opt/aware
# and /root/aware-data, (c) ensuring GPU device access for the
# unprivileged user, (d) re-testing on Modal. None of those are
# trivial. The risk-vs-effort tradeoff is acceptable for an
# ephemeral job runner; the gateway + coordinator images (which
# DO run as non-root `aware`) are the long-running surfaces and
# already follow the convention. Re-evaluate if Modal changes
# their pod security model.
DS-0002
